.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt LIBOES 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm liboes
.Nd userspace library for Endpoint Security Capabilities
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In liboes.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library provides convenience APIs for interacting with
.Pa /dev/oes ,
subscribing and muting, reading messages, and responding to authorization
events.
The library and kernel interface require an LP64 kernel and LP64 clients;
32-bit processes are not supported.
.Pp
The
.Fn oes_client_create_descendants
constructor creates a visibility-restricted client analogous to Apple's
.Fn es_new_descendants_client :
the calling process is visible for NOTIFY events, its descendants are visible
for AUTH and NOTIFY events, and all processes outside that subtree are hidden.
The scope may also be selected before mode and subscription configuration with
.Fn oes_set_descendants_scope
and inspected with
.Fn oes_get_scope .
Unlike the Apple API, opening
.Pa /dev/oes
still requires
.Fx
device privilege because OES has no code-signing
entitlement authority.
.Pp
Per-AUTH-event deadline caps are configured with
.Fn oes_set_deadline_max
and queried with
.Fn oes_get_deadline_max .
Descendants clients may also use
.Fn oes_set_deadline_min
and
.Fn oes_get_deadline_min
to request a deadline floor.
A zero SET value removes the corresponding event override.
.Fn oes_set_deadline_miss_mode
selects fail-open or fail-closed behavior when a deadline is missed or an AUTH
message cannot be queued;
.Fn oes_get_deadline_miss_mode
queries that setting.
.Pp
The mute lists applied during initial mode setup are ordinary client state.
They can be cleared with
.Fn oes_unmute_all_processes ,
.Fn oes_unmute_all_paths ,
and
.Fn oes_unmute_all_target_paths .
.Pp
The message pointer returned by
.Fn oes_read_event
is valid only until the next read on the client.
.Fn oes_message_copy
creates an owned copy, which must be released with
.Fn oes_message_free .
.Pp
Messages contain per-client global and per-event sequence numbers.
A gap
indicates that delivery was attempted but at least one intervening event was
dropped.
AUTH-derived NOTIFY messages set
.Dv OES_MSG_FLAG_AUTH_RESULT
and expose the applied decision in
.Va em_result .
These AUTH-derived notifications report the authorization outcome before the
MAC check returns; they do not prove that the originating system call later
succeeded.
.Pp
The pre-1.0 interface currently uses API and message version 1.
Messages include kernel-captured wall-clock and thread metadata,
cached executable paths, expanded process lineage, scheduling and audit
metadata, and richer vnode attributes with nanosecond timestamps.
Consumers should inspect
.Va ep_meta_flags
and
.Va ef_meta_flags
because path, credential, filesystem, and attribute data can be unavailable
independently.
.Pp
Path mute and unmute helpers reject null or empty paths with
.Er EINVAL
and paths exceeding the ABI buffer with
.Er ENAMETOOLONG .
They never silently truncate a path or issue an ioctl for these invalid inputs.
.Sh BROKERED CLIENTS
.Fn oes_client_create_brokered
requests a global NOTIFY-only descriptor from
.Va system.OES .
The requesting program must declare
.Li attributes = ["system.oes"]
in its approved unit configuration; the provider endpoint requires that
software attribute.
The constructor owns the returned descriptor and closes its factory session.
The broker protocol is the FIDL library
.Li bsd.oes.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/oes_broker.fidl :
the constructor negotiates, makes one
.Sy OpenNotify ,
and fails with
.Er EPROTO
on a malformed reply or
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT
on a different wire version.
It never falls back to an ambient device open or transparently reconnects.
.Pp
Discovery and the request use a five-second scoped RPC budget unless the caller
already has a budget, which remains intact.
The RPC timeout is additionally capped at five seconds.
Malformed replies and failed allocations close any received descriptor.
The endpoint is provided by
.Xr BSDFilesystem 8 .
An unavailable endpoint returns the discovery error.
Delivered clients have NOTIFY-only, global event scope and cannot be transferred
again or changed to AUTH or descendant scope.
Their original opener identity is the broker worker.
Factory-session closure does not revoke the descriptor; ordinary last-close
semantics apply.
.Sh DESCRIPTOR OWNERSHIP
.Fn oes_client_create_from_fd
borrows a descriptor; destroying its wrapper leaves the descriptor open.
.Fn oes_client_adopt_fd
instead takes ownership on success and closes that descriptor on destruction.
On failure, ownership remains with the caller.
The caller must supply an OES descriptor and establish close-on-exec behavior
before adoption.
Adoption checks descriptor validity but does not verify its device type or issue
ioctls, duplicate it, change rights, or change client scope.
An attenuated descriptor need not permit configuration or query ioctls.
.Pp
Closing one duplicate does not destroy the kernel client while another remains.
Destroying a borrowed wrapper does not resolve pending AUTH requests.
Do not use or close an adopted descriptor independently of its wrapper;
serialize operations and destruction on each wrapper.
.Pp
A transferred descriptor retains the kernel identity of its original opener.
Adoption does not rebind descendant scope or self-AUTH handling to its
recipient.
.Sh SEE ALSO
